00:05:14,510 --> 00:05:30,404
And what he showed is that he has sort of they built a system where you could boot up Ubuntu on two different computers, two different times, and then at the end, sort of pause and take a hash of the entire memory of the system and it gives you sort of a bit identical version of what the memory looks like.

55
00:05:30,990 --> 00:05:49,828
This is kind of very subtle, and it like, you know, wasn't immediately like, you know, mind-blowing to me at the time, but now that I think of this, this is very, very, very hard to do because it is sort of abstracting away all of the non-determinism in the system, everything from things like system clocks, other sources of randomness, disk state, and everything, and getting back to exactly the same state that you had before.

56
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,916
This is pure deterministic execution of an entire system, of an entire Linux operating system.

57
00:05:56,366 --> 00:06:02,916
And what you can do with this, or what they did with this over several years, was build what's called a deterministic hypervisor.

58
00:06:03,086 --> 00:06:18,720
So this is a system that can run other sort of virtual machines in a completely deterministic setup, and you can use this as a test bed for all kinds of other applications and targets that can be stateful, and in fact, they can be stateful, non-deterministic, and even itself be distributed.

59
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:38,720
And the way you can test distributed systems is essentially putting a virtual network inside the VM, because you can obviously have nested virtualization, and then load up a bunch of Docker containers that have some application that you want, like a distributed database or some other sort of application, and then execute this completely unmodified targets, Written in any programming language that you choose, maybe having files in I.O.

60
00:06:38,958 --> 00:06:41,920
And just keep running this in what's called a deterministic sort of simulation environment.

61
00:06:42,062 --> 00:06:43,200
Because this is all running in a VM.

62
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:55,844
The only constraint is that you are not allowed to talk to anything outside this box, you can't access the internet, but within this simulation, you can run anything, and you can always pause at any point and you can rewind and replay and you get back exactly the same state.

63
00:06:56,494 --> 00:06:59,680
And in fact, you don't necessarily always want to get back exactly the same state.

64
00:06:59,886 --> 00:07:17,700
What you might actually want to do is you might want to say, let's get back to some intermediate state that I found interesting, and then actually change something about the system by messing with some bits in memory or providing some other kind of input, and then that's called injecting entropy, and then play forward and get a slightly different behavior than you saw before, but from a common prefix, from a common sort of starting point.

65
00:07:18,350 --> 00:07:24,100
This is sort of the you know the promise of the sort of multiverse uh uh sort of state space for doing automated testing.

66
00:07:24,622 --> 00:07:32,164
