138
00:13:37,376 --> 00:13:38,020
That's all I want to do.

139
00:13:39,886 --> 00:13:45,924
And so maybe I'll give you sort of a quick primer on what raft does, since not everyone might be familiar with that.

140
00:13:46,126 --> 00:13:49,296
There's like a famous paper on which this is based.

141
00:13:49,614 --> 00:13:57,476
So Raft is a distributed consensus protocol where you have a cluster of nodes, each node is replicating the same exact state, it's called the finite state machine.

142
00:13:57,856 --> 00:14:01,028
One of those nodes is identified as the leader, the others are followers.

143
00:14:01,696 --> 00:14:05,376
which node is the leader is sort of identified using a majority vote.

144
00:14:05,536 --> 00:14:10,308
So they need to get at least half or more of the nodes to vote for that particular node as a leader.

145
00:14:10,656 --> 00:14:23,332
And because your nodes can sort of go down because of things like network faults, new leaders can be elected, and each sort of node that gets a leader is sort of they are an um increasing term numbers, so starting at term one, then going up term two, three, et cetera.

146
00:14:23,662 --> 00:14:36,496
And only the leader can accept client rights, and then that right is broadcast to all the followers, the followers have to agree to that, and then each node keeps sort of a change log of all the rights that it has applied to its internal state so that if it crashes and reboots, it can replay all of that.

147
00:14:36,718 --> 00:14:41,764
And so this protocol is designed to be tolerant to all kinds of faults, like network partitions and node crashes.

148
00:14:42,606 --> 00:14:53,136
And so when I give that user prompt, the agent is pretty easily able to sort of identify either from this paper or from the documentation that there are some core safety properties that are expected in the RAF protocol.

149
00:14:53,216 --> 00:14:54,976
I'm not gonna explain what all of these are, but they're sort of there.

150
00:14:55,136 --> 00:15:02,948
These are the core properties, like you know, there should be only one leader at any given point of time for any given term, or the state machine of all these nodes should agree on what the state is for a given term.

151
00:15:03,936 --> 00:15:06,660
and so these properties have to be somehow asserted in the system.

152
00:15:07,376 --> 00:15:09,936
and then I can just say, okay, why don't you, I mean, I don't have to say it.

