156
00:15:23,696 --> 00:15:26,404
The state is just like sum of the running numbers, it's replicated.

157
00:15:26,576 --> 00:15:34,576
Each of these nodes have some term, and there's a change log of like what operation write operations have told it, which in this case is adding numbers, and at what term this is being added.

158
00:15:35,086 --> 00:15:54,500
And all of this is being set up automatically using the AI skills, which means that this is a test cluster of three nodes with three different Docker containers, a virtualized network, a client node, there's a property based test, kind of input generator for these random numbers, all of this being synthesized automatically, put into a Docker container, ready to go, ready to launch, and send to Antithesis to run in the cloud.

159
00:15:55,214 --> 00:15:56,672
And then we say, okay, it has run in the cloud.

160
00:15:56,878 --> 00:15:58,820
The antithesis platform will do fault injection.

161
00:15:59,022 --> 00:16:04,832
So it's going to run the same test, but it's going to do some bad things from time to time, like it may partition the network.

162
00:16:04,942 --> 00:16:07,072
It might just turn off this link and say, let's see what happens now.

163
00:16:07,310 --> 00:16:18,404
In this case, when the client sends a request to A, it's not going to be able to replicate this particular entry, and so only its state will change, sorry, its state will not change because it cannot be replicated.

164
00:16:18,702 --> 00:16:24,004
The other two nodes are now going to do a leader election for term two because they're sort of they're like we don't have a leader, we need to find a leader.

165
00:16:24,302 --> 00:16:29,156
So B asks for a vote, it can vote for itself, and so eventually B becomes the leader for term number two.

166
00:16:30,032 --> 00:16:48,132
and then at this point, B is now the leader, and so B can now start accepting client requests and start committing new entries to the change log, and then eventually when the network heals, it comes back up and it catches up, and it can uh you know A becomes a follower node for term two now and it can start replicating the same state.

167
00:16:48,990 --> 00:16:53,872
And the same thing happens if you have other faults, like if node C goes down again, these faults are being injected by antithesis, right?

168
00:16:53,952 --> 00:17:04,900
There's a fault injector doing random fault injection, node C goes down, and so uh you know these new messages that are being sent by the client only get replicated on A and B, and then when C reboots, eventually it's going to catch up.

169
00:17:05,232 --> 00:17:09,828
And then the agent has also injected these safety property assertions.

170
00:17:10,157 --> 00:17:17,472
So when the faults are turned off, it's going to check did the cluster actually converge, and it's going to make sure that the final state on each of these nodes is actually correct.

171
00:17:17,872 --> 00:17:20,228
that's the final assertion to check that the state converges.

172
00:17:23,502 --> 00:17:35,232
And now we can say run this particular simulation, but whenever you find some interesting events, also go back and do this operation I told you about earlier, which is injecting entropy and going through a fork of the multiverse and trying something else.

173
00:17:35,312 --> 00:17:40,196
So this is how the first testing unit multiverse uh sort of keeps exploring the state space.

174
00:17:40,366 --> 00:17:52,068
And eventually, if you do this many, many times, you might find one timeline where there's actually is a execution of that particular assertion, which shows that hey, this assertion failed, and maybe there's a safety violation.

175
00:17:52,366 --> 00:17:54,500
And you can go and check what that safety violation looks like.

176
00:17:54,798 --> 00:18:01,632
The safety violation in this case, it seems, is that these three nodes did not actually agree on the state, even though they all have the same sort of term number.

177
00:18:01,806 --> 00:18:09,348
You can see that node A thinks the state is 49, while B and uh and so does node C, but node B thinks the state is 47.

178
00:18:09,632 --> 00:18:12,352
three out of the five core safety properties in this case are violated.

179
00:18:12,622 --> 00:18:15,108
This is a true bug that we found in the upstream raft implementation.

180
00:18:15,982 --> 00:18:18,912
And then you might ask, okay, why did this bug actually like how did you get to this point?

181
00:18:19,022 --> 00:18:20,100
This is kind of crazy.

182
00:18:20,622 --> 00:18:21,072
this is a bug.

183
00:18:21,152 --> 00:18:21,956
Can you explain it to me?

184
00:18:22,192 --> 00:18:23,232
Well, guess what?

185
00:18:23,430 --> 00:18:24,196
we got a time machine.

186
00:18:24,462 --> 00:18:35,044
So we can go back to the multiverse, and the agent is going to roll back time, go back in time to a previous state, and it's gonna play it forward and show you all the logs, and you can pause at any point and you can debug what's going on.

187
00:18:35,278 --> 00:18:37,668
And it in turns out I can explain what this bug actually looks like.

188
00:18:38,112 --> 00:18:41,732
this is a situation where initially the state is consistent on all the nodes.

189
00:18:41,998 --> 00:18:49,872
There's some network fault, like all the links are down, and in this case node C is the leader, and we are right now in term number four.

190
00:18:50,030 --> 00:18:54,596
So both node A and B try to I guess be a leader for term number four.

191
00:18:55,072 --> 00:19:02,192
eventually the network comes back, and node C can only vote for one node, so it votes for node A, and it says, okay, I'm going to give you the vote.

192
00:19:02,382 --> 00:19:05,124
But it turns out this network was jammed, so the vote is kind of slow to arrive.

193
00:19:05,486 --> 00:19:11,300
Because it's slow to arrive, each node has a timer also for how long it can wait, because the nodes have to be impatient.

194
00:19:11,566 --> 00:19:14,752
And so B becomes impatient and says, Okay, nobody's winning an election for term four.

195
00:19:14,862 --> 00:19:21,472
I'm going to now become a candidate for term number five, the new term, and it says, you know, I'm going to vote for myself, and then I ask node C for the vote.

196
00:19:21,806 --> 00:19:24,912
Node C says, yeah, I haven't voted for anybody in term five, I'll vote for you.

197
00:19:25,230 --> 00:19:30,820
And so node B gets two votes from itself and from C, and node B now becomes the leader for term number five.

198
00:19:31,342 --> 00:19:38,340
The vote for term number four is kind of slow to reach, and eventually does reach node A, and node A now also thinks it's the leader for term number four.

199
00:19:38,766 --> 00:19:39,792
So far, this is actually not bad.

200
00:19:39,872 --> 00:19:41,924
The RAF protocol is designed to handle this kind of situation.

201
00:19:42,190 --> 00:19:51,844
It's called a stale leader, and it's totally fine because even though node A thinks it's the leader for an older term, it cannot commit any new entries because it cannot get a quorum of nodes to agree for whatever it does.

202
00:19:52,334 --> 00:19:52,804
So that's fine.

203
00:19:53,454 --> 00:19:55,172
But what happens next is the problem.

204
00:19:55,470 --> 00:20:01,540
Now, node A is getting a new client request, which ordinarily it'll accept and try to replicate and it won't make any progress.

205
00:20:01,806 --> 00:20:07,748
At the same time, it's also getting this heartbeat message from node B saying, I'm the new leader, we're actually in term five, you've got to update your state.

206
00:20:08,238 --> 00:20:15,748
And both of these things, independently, if they were to apply, node A will update its term number to five and also then start rejecting client requests.

207
00:20:16,032 --> 00:20:20,676
But when they both arrive at the same time, there's a race condition in the implementation because of a data race.

208
00:20:20,942 --> 00:20:29,508
And it turns out that node A does something bad, which is it updates its term number to five, but also accepts the client request, which it cannot do because it's not the leader for term number five.

209
00:20:29,870 --> 00:20:37,188
And then it actually accepts that request, sends it to C, and then B might actually put some other data value in the same slot, and all hell breaks loose.

210
00:20:37,392 --> 00:20:38,980
This whole cluster has now diverged.

211
00:20:39,232 --> 00:20:47,396
So this is a violation of what the RAF protocol says, because the formally verified protocol does not have race conditions because you're not supposed to actually handle two different requests concurrently like that.

212
00:20:47,886 --> 00:20:49,828
And so this is actually a bug in the implementation.

213
00:20:50,030 --> 00:20:51,684
And we could replay all of this using the time machine.

214
00:20:52,558 --> 00:20:55,488
And you can see that at the same term, same index, you have sort of different values.

215
00:20:55,888 --> 00:20:57,328
that's the assertion failure.

216
00:20:58,350 --> 00:21:05,808
The other cool thing you can do is you can say, okay, uh if I if I didn't understand how all this works, can you tell me what series of operations causes this bug to be more likely than others?

217
00:21:06,062 --> 00:21:16,868
And what you can do is the agent can go back in time and run a limited sort of fuzzing exploration and check if I start from different interesting moments, what fraction of timelines actually do exhibit the same sort of safety violation.

218
00:21:17,326 --> 00:21:20,608
And that's what's called sort of causal experimentation or causality analysis.

219
00:21:20,688 --> 00:21:27,648
And it can tell you that when you have let's say stale leader election, which is supposed to be fine, but 30% of the cases you actually will end up seeing this violation.

220
00:21:27,886 --> 00:21:36,900
Now, if you actually get a client request, that probability goes up to 70%, and then you get an async heartbeat with all this thing set up, now you're very, very likely to actually hit that particular bug.

221
00:21:37,134 --> 00:21:42,048
So you can do this sort of causal experimentation for any event of interest because of the time machine.

222
00:21:44,302 --> 00:21:51,140
The other thing that I really like about this whole time travel thing is you can search over these interesting events all over the multiverse.

223
00:21:51,534 --> 00:22:02,276
So another thing I can ask my agent to do is to say, hey, use the search API that and Dithesis provides to find other events in the multiverse, not just this timeline, but other places where the state leader election happens.

224
00:22:02,448 --> 00:22:05,700
Because I just saw one of these safety violations, what happens in other cases?

225
00:22:06,126 --> 00:22:08,356
Does this happen in any other branches of the multiverse?

226
00:22:09,102 --> 00:22:10,532
And so we can do that.

227
00:22:10,990 --> 00:22:15,844
So to do that, let me first just explain what I mean by state leader election accepting client requests.

228
00:22:16,622 --> 00:22:20,644
this is how, if I as a human were to explain it to you, this is the definition that I would provide.

229
00:22:21,774 --> 00:22:23,972
notice I did not provide this to the agent, this is just me telling you.

230
00:22:24,448 --> 00:22:29,888
what I actually wanted to do is to say at some time T1, you have a node X that wins an election for some term Q.

231
00:22:30,062 --> 00:22:33,636
Then at a future time, another node wins an election for an older term P.

232
00:22:33,728 --> 00:22:38,048
So node Y is like the stale leader because it won an election for a term P which is less than Q.

233
00:22:38,350 --> 00:22:40,368
And then also node Y accepts a client request later.

234
00:22:40,558 --> 00:22:43,236
That's kind of the scenario we saw in the bug.

235
00:22:43,694 --> 00:22:48,676
And all I have to do the agent is to say, hey, find me instances in the multiverse where there's a state leader election.

236
00:22:48,768 --> 00:22:49,764
I didn't explain any of this.

237
00:22:50,608 --> 00:22:58,564
And it's going to have to look over all of these different histories of different timelines and figure out did this particular sequence of events ever occur.

238
00:22:58,830 --> 00:23:07,328
Now, this is actually not trivial because you know you can't just have all the log messages and you know put them in a SQL database and kind of do this query because it's a complex temporal query.

239
00:23:07,408 --> 00:23:11,620
So you need a nice abstraction or a nice language to sort of express these kinds of queries.

240
00:23:12,110 --> 00:23:14,244
And antithesis provides a very nice abstraction for this.

241
00:23:14,478 --> 00:23:26,820
So all of these events that are being generated during this multiverse exploration are getting stored in a database which is a tree-structured database that has these relationships between different events and what the histories are.

242
00:23:27,168 --> 00:23:34,048
And the first class query operator, just like you have sort of seen a select queries in SQL, the first class query operator in this database is a fold function.

243
00:23:34,128 --> 00:23:36,612
It's a fold down from starting from the root node down to the leaves.

244
00:23:37,294 --> 00:23:40,128
And to explain what fold looks like, so fold has a signature that looks like this.

245
00:23:40,208 --> 00:23:41,168
So it's a function.

246
00:23:41,248 --> 00:23:44,388
If you're a functional programmer, you've probably seen this before.

247
00:23:44,768 --> 00:24:00,928
you have an initial state, and then the way the function works is that you take the state from the previous ancestor node and an event that you're actually observing, you do some computation, and then you emit some set of other events as output, and then you have a new state that gets propagated down the tree.

248
00:24:01,102 --> 00:24:03,876
And you bootstrap all of this with an initial state that starts at the root node.

249
00:24:04,718 --> 00:24:11,428
And to give you an example of how this works with the simpler query before we go to state leader election, let's just say if I wanted to count elections, that's all.

250
00:24:11,918 --> 00:24:17,568
So I can write a for function, which basically says, given an event, check if the event's message is election one.

251
00:24:17,728 --> 00:24:20,644
That's that's what the system does when it wins an election, and increment a counter.

252
00:24:21,248 --> 00:24:26,052
And pass this counter down as the state to the tree, and the initial count is zero.

253
00:24:26,638 --> 00:24:39,408
And if you do that, and you run the for function, the qu the the database query is gonna sort of run along the tree, it's gonna keep counting where it sees election one, and you'll have different results at different leaf nodes based on how many elections you've seen in that particular timeline.

254
00:24:39,694 --> 00:24:46,820
And there's another operator called reduce that will help you find aggregate stats like which timeline had the max or total number of events or so on and so forth.

255
00:24:48,270 --> 00:24:55,680
So going back to the leader election, the stale leader election, if I wanted to see stale leader election accepting a client request, the fold is a little bit more complicated.

256
00:24:55,982 --> 00:25:03,108
I don't expect you to read through the entire code over here, the code snippet here, but this is something that the agent can synthesize pretty trivially.

257
00:25:04,014 --> 00:25:14,308
what it's basically doing is it's filtering again on these election one events and it's maintaining a running state of who the latest leader was based on which node won the last election and what term it won it in.

258
00:25:14,702 --> 00:25:22,180
And then if you won an election but your term was actually less than that of the latest leader, it's going to mark you as the stale leader, and that's gonna be another state.

259
00:25:22,414 --> 00:25:29,988
And both the stale leader state and the latest leader state are initialized to null, and you're gonna keep tracking this as you fold down the tree and propagate that particular state forward.

260
00:25:30,286 --> 00:25:41,700
And then if you are in a state where the stale leader is not null and you see an incoming client request, then you're gonna emit that particular event out and say, this is the event I actually care about, because I have a stale leader election and I'm seeing a client request come in.

261
00:25:42,414 --> 00:25:55,396
And then I can run this full query on top of the event history, and it's gonna keep tracking different states where you're different latest leaders, where you're different state leaders, and it's gonna say there are two timelines where I actually saw this particular state leader election and a client request.

262
00:25:55,982 --> 00:26:12,452
And so this is an extremely powerful abstraction that you can use to do all sorts of cool temporal search queries, and I'm doing some research on what are some you know cool uh sort of uh cool kinds of temporal relationships that are interesting to extract out of these multiverses that can help us with improving our bug finding ability.

263
00:26:14,606 --> 00:26:29,680
so to summarize what what antithesis has sort of enabled, you know, we can have an agent just uh you know infer safety properties automatically from documentation, from papers, set up a test workload completely automatically with an input generator, with a whole cluster of nodes using Docker containers, a virtual network.

264
00:26:29,902 --> 00:26:33,600
We can do fuzzing with chaos testing, so you have a multiverse of different timelines.

265
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:37,540
you can do fault injection, identify violations of safety properties.

266
00:26:38,080 --> 00:26:43,140
if you find any issue, you can do what's called multiverse debugging, which is go back in time, rewind and replay from any moment.

267
00:26:43,520 --> 00:26:56,228
You can understand the causal history of any particular event by going back in time and doing limited explorations, and you can search over event histories using a database that is sort of as as as fold as sort of the native query language.

268
00:26:56,718 --> 00:27:04,880
So I find all of this like you know incredibly cool, and and that's sort of why uh you know I am excited to be you know doing research with this kind of technology.

269
00:27:05,678 --> 00:27:11,236
So now that I've explained all of this, um, you know, I sort of told you what was cooking in terms of uh you know what already existed out there.

270
00:27:11,520 --> 00:27:21,572
Um you know, I sort of was reflecting when I decided to give this talk on like you know how what sort of message would I want to bring back because this is an academic conference, I'm also an academic.

271
00:27:21,934 --> 00:27:27,364
what are the kinds of things I would want to tell my students after having seen all of this actually work in production and seeing all of these capabilities?

272
00:27:28,078 --> 00:27:42,020
And so the next few slides are like you know, highly personal opinions, maybe slightly controversial, so bear with me, just letting you know these are personal opinions after being sort of frustrated with some kinds of uh you know research that I've struggled with before seeing all of this.

273
00:27:43,534 --> 00:27:57,680
So the first thing I want to say is that I personally would love to see more of academia trying to confront the fact that all of these kinds of technologies do exist out there, and they may not always show up in the academic conferences we go to, but they do exist.

274
00:27:57,934 --> 00:28:03,600
And so when you select a new problem to say, I'm gonna try to solve something, uh to at least try to do two things.

275
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:12,772
One is make as few assumptions as possible about the system under test, and try to make as many assumptions as you can about the power of the AI tools that a user is actually using.

276
00:28:13,120 --> 00:28:29,220
Because in many cases, many of the problems that I've sort of seen uh you know being being sort of addressed in in academic research, you know, if you just imagine a powerful enough AI and and in a context for your few assumptions, you actually end up sort of evolving the kind of problem that you might actually want to work on.

277
00:28:29,582 --> 00:28:46,052
And so, in terms of the research papers, it would be cool if we can sort of have better justifications of what kind of context some proposed technique is expected to work in and sort of vigorously justify why that is actually realistic and what kind of evidence there is for that problem actually to exist in the real world.

278
00:28:46,800 --> 00:28:56,868
the second thing is, based on the figure I showed you earlier about the comparison with baselines, is I think we need to sort of upgrade our notion of what a baseline would be if somebody proposes a new technique.

279
00:28:57,200 --> 00:29:06,880
This is a little bit challenging to do because it's sort of you know it's very natural to say I'm gonna compare whatever I do to a technique that I saw you know in the oopslaw or Istra that I went to three months ago.

280
00:29:06,960 --> 00:29:20,836
But you've got to sort of think about the fact that if you went to this conference three months ago, this paper was accepted like six months ago, which means it was submitted about a year ago, maybe you resubmit it a couple of times, so the people who built it got the idea initially around two years ago, and that's likely not gonna be the state of the art.

281
00:29:21,870 --> 00:29:32,272
And so we sort of got to upgrade our notion of what a baseline is and not just think about prototypes that we see in artifacts from academic papers, but basically just say, here's the problem that I'm working on, go ask your favorite agent.

282
00:29:32,432 --> 00:29:35,392
What are the kinds of tools that are available out there to solve this problem?

283
00:29:35,662 --> 00:29:40,772
Go scan blog posts, go scan hacker news, and find something that's a little bit more realistic in what people actually use out there.

284
00:29:41,230 --> 00:29:48,420
And so I think both authors and reviewers in the academic research context should be a little bit more bold in recognizing these kind of capabilities that actually are out there.

285
00:29:51,086 --> 00:30:06,116
The other thing I've been telling my students a lot as well is to be aware of this thing was called the spotlight effect, which is that it's easy to improve on things that we can measure easily, like for example, the benchmarks that we have available to us or the test paths that we have available to us.

286
00:30:06,670 --> 00:30:09,872
And that kind of leaves out a lot of holes in things that we actually cannot target out there.

287
00:30:10,062 --> 00:30:16,644
The real challenge often is not just like making the number go up slightly, but actually targeting things that are completely outside the spotlight that we actually see.

288
00:30:17,152 --> 00:30:34,512
And I actually had a PhD student who did his entire thesis on trying to improve applicability of research, and it was an uphill battle for him to get some of his papers accepted through peer review, because his whole premise was like I can actually do more if I just like look outside the box, but then I have no baselines to compare against because nobody else can even do anything.

289
00:30:34,592 --> 00:30:35,748
The number is like zero out there.

290
00:30:36,192 --> 00:30:41,092
and that's a that's a challenging thing uh to challenging position to sort of take on in our current academic context.

291
00:30:41,712 --> 00:30:51,780
Um and this is this is this is based on the observation that a lot of the successes that I've seen uh you know in in Tithesis is sort of ability to commercialize these techniques.

292
00:30:52,078 --> 00:30:57,668
It's not because of like coming up with necessarily fancy new algorithms and like fuzzing or property-based testing or fault injection.

293
00:30:57,806 --> 00:31:02,916
It's sort of building on many decades of cool uh academic work, you know, which I've also been part of this community.

294
00:31:03,152 --> 00:31:11,232
But, but I think the crux has been in coming up with platforms that allow all of these techniques to be deployed effectively with as few assumptions as possible.

295
00:31:11,232 --> 00:31:23,472
about, sort of, the system under test, everything from a deterministic hypervisor, you know, integrating with things like just Docker containers, and making no assumptions about what programming language you're actually working with, having an elastic sort of compute resource for doing these sort of fuzzing runs.

296
00:31:23,552 --> 00:31:29,872
We often ignore some of these aspects in sort of academic approaches to these things.

297
00:31:30,062 --> 00:31:33,188
Things like database for just storing logs of the things that you find.

298
00:31:33,952 --> 00:31:48,804
I haven't really seen this much, but it's actually very, very important to figure out what do you do with the data that you actually collect when you apply your automated testing techniques, and how do you sort of expose that via a command line interface or an API to agents so that the powerful agents can make the most out of the capabilities that you're building.

299
00:31:48,974 --> 00:31:49,956
And I think that's actually very, very important.

300
00:31:51,952 --> 00:32:03,312
harping on this a little bit more, sort of reflecting again on my former student who struggled with this notion of applicability a lot, we got a lot of feedback that you know there's a difference between research and engineering, right?

301
00:32:03,502 --> 00:32:08,992
We often tend to treat in academia research is like the important thing, and engineering is sort of the second class citizen, right?

302
00:32:09,072 --> 00:32:13,604
Like all of the things outside the spotlight is just like somebody has to figure out how to build a system.

303
00:32:14,030 --> 00:32:19,172
And so if you have a new algorithm or a heuristic, that's interesting, whereas building a platform or infrastructure is like second class.

304
00:32:20,078 --> 00:32:25,508
I would argue now, in the age of LLMs, that we sort of actually need to flip this around a little bit.

305
00:32:25,742 --> 00:32:30,916
Because if you think about it, uh coming up with algorithms and heuristics is something that actually can be automated pretty easily.

306
00:32:31,072 --> 00:32:34,832
It's actually very easy to iterate on new kinds of heuristics and algorithms and make the number go up.

307
00:32:35,086 --> 00:32:44,004
What's actually hard is to come up with good abstractions and platforms and infrastructures to make this actually work in a context that is really, really useful to someone who actually cares about this.

308
00:32:44,590 --> 00:32:48,356
And I think that's actually where the core research challenges are, in my opinion.

309
00:32:51,792 --> 00:32:52,032
right.

310
00:32:53,134 --> 00:32:59,472
so I sort of told you about what sort of exists out there and this is, I told you about what you know, I think about in about you know my thoughts on academia versus industry.

311
00:32:59,758 --> 00:33:09,632
I'm just gonna end this talk with a little bit of a sneak preview of the kinds of research projects that I'm sort of working on over there during my sabbatical, which I'm sort of excited about.

312
00:33:10,990 --> 00:33:23,428
The first thing I think is this time travel capability provides us with some really cool new techniques that don't really exist when you don't have time travel, which is the fact that you can do causal experiments and not just rely on correlational studies.

313
00:33:23,758 --> 00:33:41,956
So there are things that I have been studying for a long time, and the community has been studying for a long time, things like the test article problem, things like test adequacy, how do we compare something like code coverage versus mutation testing as a way of assessing test suites, things like heuristics for using dynamic analysis in guiding fuzzers.

314
00:33:42,542 --> 00:33:52,772
Often the way we have studied this in the past is by doing observational studies on open source targets, because we all know correlation is not causation, but actually when you have time travel, you actually can get causation.

315
00:33:52,974 --> 00:34:11,808
And so you can actually try things where you have a multiverse of possibilities that you explore, you rewind, go back in time, you perform some intervention, a new hypothesis that you have, and actually try out that hypothesis by executing from a fork in the multiverse, and because you have a shared history in the timeline, you actually eliminate a lot of the confounders.

316
00:34:11,888 --> 00:34:21,060
And so you can get some confidence in terms of causal relationships, which is stronger than just correlation relationships using this kind of technique.

317
00:34:22,510 --> 00:34:32,768
I'm also investigating using those temporal relationships to identify what kinds of temporal relationships might actually be useful for improving sort of fuzzer guidance going forward.

318
00:34:33,007 --> 00:34:43,364
so if you've done any sort of uh uh you're sort of familiar with anything in the space of concurrency, you might have heard of something called the happens before relation, which came out of like lamport clocks, uh, think back in the 70s.

319
00:34:43,728 --> 00:34:59,108
so I'm looking at something called happens between relation, which is uh which is a triple of three events, where you actually have like some some event A that sets up some state and some event C that might like regularize that state, but some other event B that comes in the middle and does something bad and you didn't really expect it to do so.

320
00:34:59,328 --> 00:35:09,008
And so one example is what I showed you earlier where you have a stale election and this heartbeat would actually come and tell you that okay, you actually uh you're a stale leader and you should give up.

321
00:35:09,294 --> 00:35:15,588
But what if a new client request comes in the middle and you haven't thought about handling that particular case properly?

322
00:35:15,822 --> 00:35:31,008
And so um you know, exercise for the reader or the reader's agent is to write a four function that can capture this sort of generalized regular expression and see how that helps with things like bug localization or further guidance.

323
00:35:32,288 --> 00:35:36,288
another thing that I've been working on is bringing mutation testing into industry.

324
00:35:36,494 --> 00:35:37,408
Finally, maybe.

325
00:35:37,808 --> 00:35:51,328
this is something that academics have long pursued as a means of assessing test suite quality, which is like if you inject artificial bugs in your system, you can check that the test inputs and the assertions actually make sense.

326
00:35:51,488 --> 00:36:00,164
This is, I think, more and more important as we have AI tools that are actually inferring the properties and writing the assertions for you in the first place.

327
00:36:00,608 --> 00:36:05,636
In this case, what I was working on, and we actually published a blog article on this.

328
00:36:05,648 --> 00:36:34,564
uh, last week, is doing agentic mutation testing for distributed safety properties, which means not just doing syntactic changes where I just change a plus to a minus or a true to a false, but asking an agent to understand the context of a distributed system and asking it to inject bugs that induce subtle race conditions, which fail your core safety properties like consensus in some subset of edge cases, and then checking that your automated testing setup and workload can actually catch those kinds of issues.

329
00:36:34,990 --> 00:36:42,788
so you inject those bugs and you check that with those particular bugs, you actually are able to catch the safety violation, but when you remove those bugs, your assertion passes.

330
00:36:42,928 --> 00:36:47,168
That's how you sort of gain confidence in these automatically generated properties.

331
00:36:49,390 --> 00:36:55,044
so I'm again going to sort of summarize and end with the same slide because I really like this sort of zoomed out view.

332
00:36:56,334 --> 00:37:00,208
you know, the field of automated testing has changed quite a bit in the last couple of years.

